            "image": "shopify://shop_images/BlissThera-homeostasis-therapy-phytochemcial-synergy-forest-terpenes.jpg",
            "caption": "",
            "heading": "",
            "text": "<p>In Japan it is called Shinrin-Yoku, forest bathing. The term was coined by the Japanese Forestry Agency in 1982, and since then researchers in Japan, Korea, Europe, and the United States have followed people into forests and measured what happens.</p><p><strong>What the research has measured.</strong> In 2010, Park and colleagues published results from field experiments across 24 forests in Japan, with 280 participants, comparing time in forest and urban settings. Forest settings were associated with lower salivary cortisol, lower pulse rate, lower blood pressure, and higher parasympathetic nerve activity compared with city settings (Park et al., Environmental Health and Preventive Medicine, 2010). Li and colleagues reported that natural killer cell activity in participants was higher after forest trips, and attributed part of that to phytoncides, the volatile compounds trees release (Li, Environmental Health and Preventive Medicine, 2010). Systematic reviews since then (Hansen, Jones, and Tocchini, 2017; Wen et al., 2019) have found consistent short-term physiological findings across studies, and have also noted that most studies are small, that long-term effects are less studied, and that separating the contribution of forest air from the contribution of walking, quiet, and time away is difficult.</p><p><strong>What the forest releases.</strong> Trees and understory plants emit terpenes and other volatile organic compounds: alpha-pinene and beta-pinene from conifers, limonene from citrus and many evergreens, linalool from lavender and many flowers, beta-caryophyllene from black pepper and clove. These are the compounds that give a forest its smell, and they are the subject of most phytoncide research.</p><p><strong>What BlissThera® does with it.</strong> BlissThera® practices Forest Medicine in its clinics in &nbsp;Arkansas and its affiliates, using its own patented technology, and makes its own forest botanical formulas under the oversite of Medical Director, Brian Nichol MD. You can read about the clinic at <a href=\"https://www.blissthera.com/\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"https://www.blissthera.com/\">blissthera.com</a>.</p><p><strong>Sources</strong><br/><br/>Park BJ, Tsunetsugu Y, Kasetani T, Kagawa T, Miyazaki Y. The physiological effects of Shinrin-yoku (taking in the forest atmosphere or forest bathing): evidence from field experiments in 24 forests across Japan. Environ Health Prev Med. 2010;15(1):18-26.<br/><br/>Li Q. Effect of forest bathing trips on human immune function. Environ Health Prev Med. 2010;15(1):9-17.<br/><br/>Hansen MM, Jones R, Tocchini K. Shinrin-Yoku (Forest Bathing) and Nature Therapy: A State-of-the-Art Review. Int J Environ Res Public Health. 2017;14(8):851.<br/><br/>Wen Y, Yan Q, Pan Y, Gu X, Liu Y. Medical empirical research on forest bathing (Shinrin-yoku): a systematic review. Environ Health Prev Med. 2019;24:70.</p><p>These statements have not been evaluated by the Food and Drug Administration. BlissThera® products are not intended to diagnose, treat, cure, or prevent any disease.</p>",
